customer, how does your product or

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:31.250
brand get used by the user, the customer
to attain a goal, their goal and

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:32.250
transform their life.

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:34.650
And so, you're really anchoring so it's
not really about you,

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:37.720
it's not about the team but it's really
about this one person.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:43.500
Any other sort of tips on really
developing that user story so

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:46.140
that it persuades you like, yep this is
gonna be big.

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:51.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean I think there's always,
you know, look, in the end there's no

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:55.570
single great business that's been built on
a single customer.

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:56.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:57.940
So, there's always a starting point,

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:02.810
which is I have, I have a customer who is
using this and

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:05.840
that is sufficiently gauging to get you
the next customer, et cetera.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:07.760
I always think of this.

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:11.030
Particularly, in the start up world, I
talk about this.

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:13.680
The fact that start ups have any customers
is miraculous.

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:16.640
You should never work with a start up.

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:20.210
Start ups are, you know, they likely will
fail and go out of business.

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:22.630
They have technical solutions that are
unproven.

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:26.610
There are all sorts of reasons why if you
are a big company-

00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:30.090
But, in the enterprise space, you wouldn't
use a, a,

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:34.370
a start-up's product, which is crazy.

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; If you're a consumer and

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:37.553
you're all ready on Facebook.

00:26:37.553 --> 00:26:40.720
No, or better yet, you're all ready on
MySpace, why do you go to Facebook?

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:44.050
Why do you start using Twitter if you all
ready have a thing.

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:48.500
Why are you using WhatsApp instead of, you
know, Messenger or whatever.

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:52.990
So, the momentum is a 100% against
start-ups.

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:57.270
And so, the story, this, this capacity to
tell the tale of

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:01.800
here's how we got someone who should not
have used our product to use our product.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:04.562
It's always powerful.

00:27:04.562 --> 00:27:08.728
And the, and the more that customer is
known to be terrible-

00:27:08.728 --> 00:27:10.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; The better.

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:14.610
I won't use any names necessarily, but
there are certain businesses in

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:17.770
Silicon Valley that are known to be
impossible customers.

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:18.320
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:22.290
And so, when you come to me and picture
business or

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:27.400
better yet, you go to a smaller company
and say oh, who are your customers.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:30.670
Oh, I have this particular customer.

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:32.148
They say, how did that happen?

00:27:32.148 --> 00:27:32.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Like why-

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:34.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; You know, why are they using you?

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:40.520
So, so, it is a snowball effect that it,
that is, is incredibly powerful.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:41.960
And, you should understand that.

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:43.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; You should understand that,

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:46.750
that the message of each customer is a
message of

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:49.590
solving problems in the real world.

00:27:49.590 --> 00:27:53.890
And, the story isn't, oh, I have a, a, I'm
embedded in blah.

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:56.480
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; It's, so-and-so had a problem that

00:27:57.680 --> 00:28:00.740
they couldn't solve with the obvious, you
know,

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:02.880
I couldn't go to Oracle and solve this
problem.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:05.020
I couldn't use Facebook and solve this
problem.

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:10.460
And so, when they looked for the solution,
the solution came clear despite

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:13.420
the fact that it was little and unproven
and all this stuff.

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:15.860
It was, oh, my god, this is so much
better.

00:28:15.860 --> 00:28:17.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I have to try it.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:17.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:22.480
It's a fantastic point, just this notion
of who, in fact, even is the user.

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:28.750
And then, the leverage, I mean, certainly
from a marketing perspective you see more

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:33.860
and more companies not just, you know, be
inspired by and reach out and

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.990
empower to tell the user's story as a part
of, you know,

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:42.900
basically marketing start up with the
company innovation.

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:44.320
Right?
You're also seeing a closer and

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:47.370
closer relationship between the user and
customer and

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:52.460
the actual company because co-development
becomes important.

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:56.800
Because some of these users are so
influential that they can become much

00:28:56.800 --> 00:29:02.280
more, you know, not only authentic and
believable than the company can, but

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:04.440
actually are more connected to the
network.

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:07.000
That the company wants to reach.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.320
&gt;&gt; Well, to, to this point, I mean this
is, and I, I,

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:11.960
I have stolen this playbook repeatedly.

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:12.950
And obviously, I don't think that,

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:16.670
so I have a company I invested in called
Splunk.

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:21.460
And, Splunk is this very nerdy tool that
allows, that takes machine data,

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:24.430
all sorts of data, and allows you to make
sense of it.

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:25.880
And, their original customers were,

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:29.770
you know, IT people are trying to keep the
computers up.

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:34.240
And then, their next set of customers were
security people trying to keep

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:38.520
the networks secure, and then compliance
people trying, very nerdy, very technical.

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:44.640
Blank very quickly discovered oh this is a
tool that solves all sorts of problems.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:47.070
And, our customers are figuring out what
those problems are.

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:50.630
And, there's no better way to get the next
customer then to say,

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:55.340
oh, this customer did this, and this, and
this, but it turns out there's

00:29:56.480 --> 00:30:00.770
a thing that's better than me saying, oh,
you know, this customer did x, y, and z.

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:03.051
It's getting them to tell the story.

00:30:03.051 --> 00:30:06.247
And so, Splunk has deployed their own
customers to

00:30:06.247 --> 00:30:10.051
tell these triumphant stories, and you
talked about this.

00:30:10.051 --> 00:30:10.732
This is.

00:30:10.732 --> 00:30:12.370
Make your customer the hero.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:15.241
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; This is in every instance how does

00:30:15.241 --> 00:30:20.234
someone in a big enterprise, it how were,
how were they empowered to

00:30:20.234 --> 00:30:25.658
solve some big problem and save their
company a bunch of time or money or

00:30:25.658 --> 00:30:30.940
mecha, you know, mechanize things that
seemed impossible whatever.

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:35.350
So in every instance we, as one get the
users on the stage,

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:37.860
they tell their story and we say, oh, my
God!

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:39.650
You are so smart!

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:40.330
Right?

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:42.540
But yeah, you're smart for using our
product, right?

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:43.040
[CROSSTALK].
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:43.670
&gt;&gt; That's amazing.

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:46.759
So now, in every instance, that I can make
this work I

00:30:46.759 --> 00:30:51.234
say get potential customers in the room,
whether it's a dinner [CROSSTALK].

00:30:51.234 --> 00:30:52.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:57.220
&gt;&gt; Or an auditorium and get your existing
customers to tell the story of how they,

00:30:57.220 --> 00:30:59.140
how it is making them heroic.

00:30:59.140 --> 00:30:59.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:03.420
that, there, that is the best sales
technique I've ever seen.

00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:05.183
It, it will win everyday [CROSSTALK].

00:31:05.183 --> 00:31:07.530
&gt;&gt; It have.
&gt;&gt; If you have customers who will do it.

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:10.994
&gt;&gt; Yeah, which also reminds you, pick the
right customer.

00:31:10.994 --> 00:31:12.970
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well that's helpful.

00:31:12.970 --> 00:31:14.921
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And then the fourth and

00:31:14.921 --> 00:31:19.544
sort of last sort of story that is
certainly important from David's,

00:31:19.544 --> 00:31:24.610
perspective in, in his world is, you know,
the reason why this is huge, you know?

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:28.040
So you're using in this one, you know,
both data, to trend data, you know,

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:32.100
data from, you know, how a companies doing
and then where you're architecting

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:37.250
a narrative and organizing framework so
that the venture capitalist is

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:41.170
making the inference in his or her own
head this is gonna be huge.

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:42.380
Any comments on this story?

00:31:43.490 --> 00:31:44.541
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean it, you need it.

00:31:44.541 --> 00:31:45.088
[CROSSTALK].

00:31:45.088 --> 00:31:45.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; In the venture business.

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:47.190
I mean, it's funny.

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:51.120
I say to people look, in the end, venture
capital is not for everyone.

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:54.520
Venture capital comes with it, a price
which is that if I'm

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:59.280
gonna invest $10 million in your business,
your business actually has to be very big.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:02.330
To make it, my piece worth more than 10
million dollars.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:02.910
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:04.260
&gt;&gt; So when you come to me,

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:08.200
to tell me about your exciting technology,
that's store.

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:12.140
You, you know, first of all, it has to be,
I'm solving a very acute problem, and

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:14.510
I can get those first set of customers.

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:16.709
&gt;&gt; RIght.
&gt;&gt; But, then simultaneously,

00:32:16.709 --> 00:32:19.022
the world is our oyster, right?

00:32:19.022 --> 00:32:22.826
There's some huge opportunity here that is
transformative.

00:32:22.826 --> 00:32:27.166
And, and you have to believe both stories,
and that's a very tricky thing.

00:32:27.166 --> 00:32:30.635
And great entrepreneurs are very good at
that, about this like,

00:32:30.635 --> 00:32:32.160
here's what we're doing.

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:33.480
That's the boring stuff.

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:36.920
The really exciting stuff is that when I
have a 100 million users,

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:40.320
you know, then it's, then think about
this, right?

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.065
I mean we funded a company called Wattpad.

00:32:43.115 --> 00:32:47.540
Wattpad is this consumer experience where
you, where people are uploading stories

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:51.770
and reading stories on their, la, largely
on their mobile devices.

00:32:51.770 --> 00:32:54.920
The founders thought this was important,
they care about stories,

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:59.460
they care about the, the, the written
word, you know, but in the beginning when

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:03.910
it's just you know, hundreds of people
doing that, they say, oh, that's great!

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:04.450
You, you, know? [CROSSTALK]. &gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:06.770
&gt;&gt; Some people reading some stories or
whatever.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:10.180
But now when it's tens of millions of
people and

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:16.100
there are hundreds of thousands of stories
being uploaded on a daily basis and

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:20.030
billions of minutes a month being spent
consuming content, being reading.

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:23.590
We're talking about people reading.

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:26.070
It's pretty easy to sell that big story,
right?

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:29.665
You have folks like Margaret Atwood saying
like, this is important.

00:33:29.665 --> 00:33:33.855
Wattpad is important for the
transformation of, of the reading world,

00:33:33.855 --> 00:33:38.401
that kids still read, that people still
care about the written word, right?

00:33:38.401 --> 00:33:38.901
[CROSSTALK].
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:38.901 --> 00:33:40.491
&gt;&gt; So the story starts with,

00:33:40.491 --> 00:33:45.480
I have a technical solution that makes it
easy to upload stories and consume them,

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:50.248
on a mobile device, but the big picture
is, you know, and, and, and as far as

00:33:50.248 --> 00:33:55.418
this team is concerned, having tens of
millions of users is the beginning, right?

00:33:55.418 --> 00:33:56.390
[CROSSTALK]. &gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And we,

00:33:56.390 --> 00:34:00.600
and, and, so, so being able to tell that
and it doesn't matter what it is, right?

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:04.946
My, my, my buddy [UNKNOWN] when he was
teaching [UNKNOWN] was,

00:34:04.946 --> 00:34:10.398
I have seen that, how hard payroll is,
I've seen what a waste of time it is,

00:34:10.398 --> 00:34:14.750
for people, we can make it better, we can
make it an asset.

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:19.830
It will transform the ability of millions
of small business owners to recon,

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:22.410
to regain control of their lives.

00:34:22.410 --> 00:34:25.750
And he's taken that one step further with
bill.com, his next company.

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:27.600
He's he's into the exciting stuff.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:29.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Start with payroll,

00:34:29.200 --> 00:34:33.830
move on to bills, same thing, how do I
manage accounts payable and receivable?

00:34:33.830 --> 00:34:34.830
Is that a big story?

